From 24c2dc7f95d280f0465ff6677f23b863f4caaa17 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 22:43:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][Player information] Processed at most four times a
 second while nothing moves

The player information is an opaque plate over most of the picture,
and many of its values change in almost every frame (the queues of the
player, its cache, the rate of the frames). Each change dirtied it,
and with it the whole GUI pass of that frame was drawn again. Where the
GUI is composited over the picture from its FBO (native Dolby Vision,
the HDR10/HLG composite, Dolby Vision rebuilt as HDR10), a frame whose
GUI is not dirty is composited from the FBO of the frame before and
its GUI pass is left out (CDVGuiReuse). The 1.0 diagnostics of an AMD
iGPU with the panel open had 0 to 129 of 240 frames so at 24 fps, and
11.3 ms of GPU time a frame with none against 6 to 7.7 ms with half of
them; a 60 Hz NVIDIA output, 540 to 580 of 600.

CPlayerInfoRefresh (dialogs/PlayerInfoRefresh.h) has the panel
processed at most every 250 ms while nothing is asked of it and
nothing of it moves, through a hook of CGUIWindow, ProcessDue(): a
window that answers false is shown and not processed, as one that
<gui><smartredraw> leaves where nothing is dirty (CGUIProcessPass
counts it shown). Its values come from the hades.* properties, which
are published at most twice a second, and from Kodi's labels: each is
shown at most 250 ms later, and the GUI pass is drawn at most four
times a second for the panel. It is processed at once after a key or a
message (the focus, opening), and in every frame while something of it
moves by itself: its window opening or closing, a text that scrolls
(CGUILabel::Scrolls(), of a label or a button), a text box that
scrolls its lines, a fade label, a multi-image. What it shows does not
change; it is drawn less often. Both of its dialogs do it (Estuary's
DialogPlayerProcessInfo, Kodi's own panel too, and the pages of Hades
for another skin), and kodi.log says at debug level, when it closes,
in how many of its frames it was processed.
